/* Theory questions from the past-paper booklet (Vinod's and Swarbrick's sections) and the
 * practice document. These appear in the Question bank alongside every oral case step.
 * Model answers were rewritten where the student version was incomplete or wrong. */
const THEORY = [
  // ---------------- RGP fitting (Vinod)
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q1', q: 'Rigid lens, Ks 7.80 @180 / 7.60 @90, BC 7.60 mm. What fit and fluorescein pattern do you expect? And Ks 7.60 @180 / 7.70 @90 with BC 7.60?',
    model: `<p><b>Case 1</b> (WTR: flat K 7.80 horizontal, steep K 7.60 vertical; BC on the steep K, so steeper than flat K): slight apical clearance; the lens bears along the flatter horizontal meridian and clears along the steeper vertical one → <b>horizontal band/dumbbell of touch at 3 and 9, pooling at 12 and 6</b>.</p><p><b>Case 2</b> (ATR: flat K 7.70 vertical, steep K 7.60 horizontal; BC 7.60): slight apical clearance, clearance at 3 and 9, bearing along the flatter vertical meridian → <b>vertical dumbbell</b>.</p><p><b>Soft lens</b> 7.60 BC on 7.60/7.70 Ks: very tight (soft lenses are fitted ~0.6–1.0 mm flatter than K). Little movement, conjunctival indentation/blanching, vision and keratometer mires clear just after a blink then blur.</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q2', q: 'How would you loosen an RGP fit, and by roughly how much for a small but clinically significant change?',
    model: `<table class="t"><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Tighten</th><th>Loosen</th></tr><tr><td>Total diameter</td><td>↑</td><td>↓</td></tr><tr><td>BOZD</td><td>↑</td><td>↓</td></tr><tr><td>BOZR</td><td>↓ (steeper)</td><td>↑ (flatter)</td></tr><tr><td>Peripheral radii</td><td>↓</td><td>↑</td></tr><tr><td>Thickness</td><td>↓</td><td>↑</td></tr></table><p>Typical steps: BOZR 0.05 mm (≈0.25 D), TD/BOZD 0.2–0.5 mm. To keep the same fluorescein pattern when changing BOZD: every 0.5 mm change in BOZD needs a 0.05 mm change in BOZR in the same direction (bigger zone → flatter BOZR).</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q3', q: 'How do tear-film forces hold a rigid lens on the eye, and what happens with excessive tearing?',
    model: `<p>A concave tear meniscus at the lens edge creates negative (suction) pressure under the lens; together with post-lens tear-film viscosity/thickness (friction) and lid forces, this centres and holds the lens. Smaller meniscus radius (dry eye) → more negative pressure → more adherent, less mobile lens. <b>Excessive tearing</b>: thicker tear film, larger meniscus radius → less negative pressure → lens lifts, moves more and decentres (often drops).</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q4', q: 'Advantages and disadvantages of apical touch, apical clearance and alignment fits.',
    model: `<ul><li><b>Alignment ("on K")</b>: even bearing, good tear circulation, minimal corneal distortion and spectacle blur. Can ride high with the lid, moderate movement, fit flattens if the cornea swells.</li><li><b>Apical clearance (steeper than K)</b>: better centration (useful for hyperopes, wide apertures). Poor tear exchange, bubbles/dimple veiling, corneal shape change.</li><li><b>Apical touch (flatter than K)</b>: lid attachment, good tear exchange. Low riding if the upper lid doesn't hold it, excessive movement, central bearing → abrasion/moulding.</li></ul><p>Alignment fit choices: TD 8.8–9.6 (≈ HVID − 2), BOZD from max pupil (<5 mm → 7.6; 5–7 → 7.8; >7 → 8.0), BOZR by toricity (spherical → on K; 0.25–1.00 D → on K or 0.25 D steeper; 1–2 D → 0.50 D steeper; >2 D → ΔK/3 steeper).</p>` },
  { topic: 'materials', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q5', q: 'What are the desirable features of a contact lens material? Compare PMMA, HEMA and RGP materials.',
    model: `<p>Optical clarity, oxygen permeability, biocompatibility, wettability, durability, dimensional stability, deposit resistance, ease of manufacture, cost.</p><ul><li><b>PMMA</b>: optically excellent, durable, easy to make, great tear exchange; Dk ≈ 0 → hypoxia, central oedema, warpage.</li><li><b>RGP</b>: 1st gen silicone acrylates (Dk to ~30; poor wettability, deposits), 2nd gen fluorosilicone acrylates (higher Dk, better deposit resistance), super/hyper-Dk (XO 100, XO2 141, Menicon Z 163).</li><li><b>HEMA (hydrogel)</b>: comfort, little corneal shape change; Dk depends on water (max ~80% water → Dk ~40–60), fragile, deposits (ionic), more demanding care, vision not as crisp. Silicone hydrogels decouple Dk from water.</li></ul>` },
  { topic: 'fitting', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q6', q: 'What anterior eye measurements do you take before fitting, and how does each affect the fit?',
    model: `<ul><li><b>Ks / topography</b>: corneal toricity (spherical vs toric BOZR), BOZR selection, shape/eccentricity (peripheral curves, aspheric designs), irregularity (keratoconus).</li><li><b>HVID</b>: diameter. RGP ≈ HVID − 2 mm; soft ≈ HVID + 2–3 mm.</li><li><b>Pupil size</b> (dim light): BOZD ≥ pupil + ~1 mm; multifocal suitability.</li><li><b>Palpebral aperture, lid position and tension</b>: lens position, lid attachment, RGP diameter, toric stability.</li><li><b>Tear film</b> (TBUT, meniscus), lids and lashes, conjunctiva, cornea baseline staining.</li></ul>` },
  { topic: 'fitting', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q7–8', q: 'What methods keep toric lenses on axis, in soft and rigid lenses?',
    model: `<p><b>Soft</b>: prism ballast (0.75–1.5Δ BD; thick inferiorly; watermelon-seed squeeze), peri-ballast (no prism in optic zone), dynamic stabilisation / double slab-off (thin top and bottom, thick at 3 and 9; most common modern designs), truncation (1–1.5 mm cut at the base; last resort).</p><p><b>Rigid</b>: spherical RGPs don't need stabilising. Front-surface torics: 1–1.5Δ base-down prism ± truncation, expect 10–15° nasal rotation. Back-surface torics and bitorics: the toric back surface aligns with the toric cornea.</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q9', q: 'Work through a spherical RGP order: spec −4.50/−2.00×180 (12 mm), Ks 42.50 @180, 44.25 @90.',
    model: `<p>Ocular Rx: −4.50 → −4.27; −6.50 → −6.04 → <b>−4.25/−1.75×180</b>. Corneal toricity 1.75 D → BOZR = flat K + ¼ΔK = 42.50 + 0.44 ≈ <b>43.00 D (7.85 mm)</b>. Tear lens: lens 0.50 D steeper than flat K → +0.50 D tear lens → BVP = −4.25 − 0.50 = <b>−4.75 D</b> (SAM: steeper add minus).</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q9', q: 'Work through a bitoric order: ocular Rx −6.00/−3.00×180, Ks 42.50 @180, 45.50 @90.',
    model: `<p>Residual astigmatism = −3.00 − (−3.00) = 0 → <b>SPE bitoric</b>. Mandell–Moore (3.00 D toricity): flat meridian 0.25 D flatter → 42.25 D (7.99 mm); steep 0.75 D flatter → 44.75 D (7.54 mm). BVP: flat meridian −6.00 + 0.25 = <b>−5.75</b>; steep meridian −9.00 + 0.75 = <b>−8.25</b> (FAP: flatter add plus). Peripheral: SCR ≈ BOZR + 1 mm, PCR ≈ SCR + 2 mm; TD 9.2, BOZD 7.8, CT ~0.15.</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q10', q: 'Define refractive, corneal, internal and residual astigmatism. How do they guide RGP design?',
    model: `<p><b>Refractive</b>: total astigmatism of the eye (refraction) = corneal + internal. <b>Corneal</b>: from the front corneal surface (keratometry/topography). <b>Internal</b>: crystalline lens, posterior cornea, tilt. <b>Residual</b>: what remains with a spherical RGP in place ≈ refractive − corneal (typically ≤0.50 D ATR).</p><p>Spherical RGP: corneal <2 D and low residual. FST: low corneal, high residual. BST: corneal >2 D and refractive ≈ 1.2–1.4 × corneal. SPE bitoric: corneal >2 D ≈ refractive. CPE bitoric: corneal >2 D ≠ refractive.</p>` },
  { topic: 'materials', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q11', q: 'Relationship between water content, permeability (Dk) and transmissibility (Dk/t) in RGP and hydrogel lenses.',
    model: `<p>Dk/t = Dk ÷ thickness. <b>Hydrogels</b>: oxygen travels in the water phase, so Dk rises exponentially with water content, capped by fragility at ~80% water. <b>RGP</b>: no water; Dk comes from siloxane/fluorine; transmissibility rises with Dk and falls with thickness. <b>SiHy</b>: Dk rises as silicone increases and water falls. Higher Dk also improves CO₂ efflux (less stromal acidosis).</p>` },
  { topic: 'rx', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q12', q: 'Why are hyperopes "happy" and myopes "miserable" when they switch to contact lenses?',
    model: `<p><b>Accommodation</b>: at 33 cm a CL wearer (or emmetrope) needs ~+2.47 D; a +10 D spectacle wearer needs ~+3.29 D; a −10 D spectacle wearer ~+1.83 D. So hyperopes accommodate less with CLs and myopes more. <b>Convergence</b>: plus specs induce base-out on near gaze (more convergence), minus specs base-in (less). Switching to CLs relieves hyperopes and loads myopes, so myopes notice presbyopia earlier. Magnification: myopes get a bigger image with CLs (better VA). Fitting high-plus RGPs is harder (heavy, low riding).</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q13', q: 'What are the advantages and disadvantages of different RGP edge profiles?',
    model: `<p><b>Shape</b>: rounded anterior edge is most comfortable (lid interaction); blunt/sharp edges uncomfortable. <b>Axial edge lift</b> ~60–90 µm: too high → lid sensation, excess movement, decentration, 3 & 9 staining (thirsty meniscus, bridging); too low → seal-off, limbal trauma, debris, adherence. <b>Edge width</b> ~0.25–0.30 mm: too wide → debris, FB sensation, excess movement; too narrow → poor tear exchange, indentation.</p>` },

  // ---------------- Physiology and complications (Swarbrick)
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Past paper · Swarbrick Q1', q: 'Compare the physical, metabolic and inflammatory effects of rigid versus soft lenses.',
    model: `<p><b>Physical</b>: rigid lenses mould the cornea (warpage, especially low-Dk/PMMA), reduce corneal sensitivity more, are less comfortable (edge-lid), have a thinner, less stable pre-lens tear film and cause 3 & 9 staining. Soft: minimal shape change, more comfortable.</p><p><b>Metabolic</b>: RGP tear pump (~10–20% exchange per blink) plus diffusion; soft relies on diffusion through the lens (~1–2% exchange). Rigid oedema is central; soft oedema is across the whole cornea.</p><p><b>Inflammatory</b>: limbal hyperaemia and vascularisation, CLARE, CLPU and infiltrates are mainly soft-lens problems; CLPC occurs with both (localised with rigid/SiHy, generalised with hydrogel).</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Past paper · Swarbrick Q3', q: 'Risk factors for CL-related microbial keratitis and how to minimise them.',
    model: `<p>Overnight wear (biggest), poor case hygiene, tap water/swimming, not washing hands, topping up solution, infrequent case replacement, smoking, internet purchase without aftercare, male, young age, high socioeconomic status (in some studies), certain solutions. Reduce: DDs instead of EW, education and written instructions, case replacement every 1–3 months, no water, review compliance at every aftercare.</p>` },
  { topic: 'care', source: 'Past paper · Swarbrick Q4', q: 'What instructions would you give a new soft lens wearer, including the cleaning procedure?',
    model: `<p>Wash and dry hands; work over a table, not the bathroom basin; right lens first; check inside-out. No sleeping, no water, no swimming without goggles + discard. DDs: discard after one use. Reusables: rub and rinse each side, fresh solution nightly, ≥ minimum soak time, never top up, case rinsed with solution and air-dried face down, case replaced every 1–3 months, discard solution after its opened date. Warning signs: redness, pain, blur, light sensitivity → remove lens, seek care. Aftercares at ~2 weeks, 1, 3, 6 months, then 6–12 monthly.</p>` },
  { topic: 'care', source: 'Past paper · Swarbrick Q5', q: 'What adverse effects come from non-compliance, and how do you reduce non-compliance?',
    model: `<p>Effects: MK, CLPU/infiltrates, CLPC, SICS, lens damage/deposits. Common errors: not rubbing, poor rinsing, topping up, poor case hygiene, overwearing, sleeping in lenses. Strategies: careful patient selection, in-office demonstration, the simplest system (or DDs), clear written instructions, check technique at every aftercare, adjust replacement schedule and review frequency.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Past paper · Swarbrick Q6', q: 'Acute and chronic hypoxic effects in epithelium, stroma and endothelium, and how to manage them.',
    model: `<p><b>Mechanism</b>: ↓O₂ → anaerobic glycolysis → lactate; CO₂ retention → stromal acidosis; osmotic load → oedema.</p><ul><li><b>Epithelium</b>: acute oedema (haloes), ↓ mitosis, ↓ sensitivity, loosened junctions; chronic microcysts (intervene if >50 or breaking through), vacuoles, thinning, ↓ adhesion.</li><li><b>Stroma</b>: oedema, striae (~5%), folds (~8–10%), haze, thinning, neovascularisation.</li><li><b>Endothelium</b>: blebs (20–30 min, transient), polymegathism/pleomorphism (permanent).</li></ul><p>Management: higher Dk/t, thinner lens, less wear, stop EW, switch to SiHy DDs. Microcysts rebound for ~1 week, then clear over 1–3 months.</p>` },
  { topic: 'materials', source: 'Past paper · Swarbrick Q7', q: 'Describe the Dk/t ranges of hydrogel, SiHy and RGP lenses and which wear schedules they suit.',
    model: `<p>Criteria: Holden–Mertz 24 (daily, no oedema), 34 (overnight 8%), 87 (overnight 4% = no lens); Harvitt–Bonanno 35 daily / 125 overnight. Hydrogels: Dk/t ~10–40 (daily wear). SiHy: ~80–175 (daily, many approved for EW: Night & Day Aqua 175, 30 nights). RGP: Dk 19 (Boston IV) to 163 (Menicon Z); hyper-Dk used for Ortho-K/overnight.</p>` },
  { topic: 'care', source: 'Past paper · Swarbrick Q8', q: 'Advantages, disadvantages and ideal patients for MPS, peroxide and heat disinfection.',
    model: `<p><b>Heat</b> (80 °C, 10 min, saline): very effective (incl. most Acanthamoeba), no preservative allergy, cheap; ages lenses, denatures/bakes protein, discolours with preserved saline, bad for high-water lenses, not portable.</p><p><b>H₂O₂</b>: very effective, preservative-free (allergic/SICS patients); needs ≥6 h neutralisation, toxic if not neutralised, compliance-dependent. Not for shift workers.</p><p><b>MPS</b>: convenient, portable, simple; least effective, preservative reactions (SICS, allergy), relies on rubbing.</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Past paper · Swarbrick Q9', q: 'Why might RGPs be healthier than hydrogels?',
    model: `<p>Greater tear exchange (10–20% vs ~1%) flushes debris and supplies oxygen; no water content to absorb preservatives/microbes; fewer deposits and easier cleaning; lower MK risk; fewer hypoxic and inflammatory events (limbal redness, vascularisation, CLARE, CLPU). Trade-offs: comfort, adaptation, 3 & 9 staining, warpage with low Dk.</p>` },
  { topic: 'lenschoice', source: 'Past paper · Swarbrick Q10', q: '"Disposable contact lenses are healthier for the eye than conventional hydrogel lenses." Discuss.',
    model: `<p>Less exposure to denatured deposits and solution chemicals (less CLPC, allergy, SICS); thinner lenses (less lid interaction); no lens ageing (aged lenses tighten, trap debris); no case with DDs. Caveat: DDs don't eliminate MK risk, but MK in DD wearers is less severe with fewer vision-loss cases.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Past paper · Swarbrick Q11', q: 'A patient complains of dry eye late in the day. What do you do?',
    model: `<p>Assess: TBUT, debris in tear film, meibomian glands, lid margins, staining, lens fit, deposits, wearing time, environment, meds. Treat MGD/bleph (warm compresses, lid hygiene, expression), preservative-free lubricants. Lens changes: optimise fit, change material (lower water hydrogel or SiHy with better wettability), DDs, reduce wear time, change solution (H₂O₂), blinking exercises. Review in ~2 weeks.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Past paper · Swarbrick Q12', q: 'How do you differentiate mucin balls, microcysts and vacuoles?',
    model: `<table class="t"><tr><th></th><th>Mucin balls</th><th>Microcysts</th><th>Vacuoles</th></tr><tr><td>After lens removal</td><td>Gone (leave pits that pool NaFl)</td><td>Persist</td><td>Persist</td></tr><tr><td>Illumination</td><td>Translucent</td><td>Reversed</td><td>Unreversed</td></tr><tr><td>NaFl</td><td>Pooling in depressions</td><td>Occasional punctate staining</td><td>No staining</td></tr></table><p>Mucin balls: SiHy, high modulus, mechanical; harmless. Microcysts: chronic hypoxia. Vacuoles: fluid-filled, hypoxia-related.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Past paper · Swarbrick Q13', q: 'Draw/describe common corneal staining patterns in CL wear and their causes.',
    model: `<ul><li><b>3 & 9 o'clock</b>: RGP peripheral desiccation.</li><li><b>Superior arcuate (SEAL)</b>: stiff SiHy + tight upper lid.</li><li><b>Diffuse punctate (SICS)</b>: solution toxicity (biguanides with some SiHys).</li><li><b>Inferior band/smile</b>: exposure, incomplete blinking, dehydrating thin hydrogel.</li><li><b>Central</b>: hypoxia, flat RGP bearing, debris under a tight lens.</li><li><b>Foreign-body tracks / linear</b>: debris under the lens, insertion trauma.</li><li><b>Dimple veil</b>: bubbles under a steep RGP.</li></ul>` },

  // ---------------- Practice document highlights
  { topic: 'history', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'What conditions make you cautious about fitting CLs, and what are absolute contraindications?',
    model: `<p><b>Caution</b>: blepharitis/MGD, pinguecula/pterygium, poor dexterity (arthritis, elderly), poor hygiene, dusty environment, allergy/atopy, skin disease (rosacea, eczema), glaucoma drops (wait 10–15 min after drops), drying medications, pregnancy, thyroid eye disease, diabetes, immunocompromise.</p><p><b>Contraindicated</b>: active eye infection or inflammation, severe dry eye, inability to follow instructions or handle lenses.</p>` },
  { topic: 'lenschoice', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'When would you choose daily disposables over monthlies?',
    model: `<p>First-time wearers, occasional or sports wear, swimming/water sports (discard after), poor compliance with care or tendency to nap in lenses, travel and outdoor activities, allergy/deposit problems, solution sensitivity, busy people wanting convenience. Monthlies when DD doesn't come in the Rx (e.g. −20.00 or −3.25 DC → Biofinity XR) or for near-daily wear with good compliance.</p>` },
  { topic: 'fitting', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'Why do we want full limbal coverage and some movement with a soft lens?',
    model: `<p><b>Limbal coverage</b>: avoid desiccation of an exposed band of cornea, avoid the edge rubbing the limbus (stem cells), reduce mechanical stress. <b>Movement</b> (~0.2–0.5 mm): tear exchange under the lens, removal of debris and metabolic waste, reduced risk of inflammatory events. Too tight → indentation, debris trapping, CLARE risk; too loose → decentration, blur after blinks, edge fluting, discomfort.</p>` },
  { topic: 'fitting', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'Where should the toric orientation mark sit, and why might a toric lens not align?',
    model: `<p>Most marks are at 6 o'clock (Biofinity, MyDay, Alcon, B+L); Acuvue Oasys uses 6 and 12; Air Optix uses 3, 6, 9. Expect 5–10° nasal rotation. Causes of misalignment: too tight (clamps off-axis), too loose (unstable), lid geometry and tension, blink pattern, oblique cyl axis, thickness profile of the design. A minus-cyl lens is thickest along its most-minus meridian: for WTR (axis ~180) that is at 12 and 6, under the lids, where lid pressure on the thick zones can fight dynamic stabilisation; for ATR (axis ~90) the thick zones sit at 3 and 9 in the aperture, which suits thin-zone designs.</p>` },
  { topic: 'specialty', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'Indications for scleral lenses, piggyback and hybrid lenses.',
    model: `<p><b>Scleral/large RGP</b>: advanced or decentred keratoconus, pellucid marginal degeneration, post-graft, severe ocular surface disease, RGP intolerance; vault the cornea. Risks: hypoxia (no tear exchange), midday fogging, seal-off, limbal stem cell stress. Also the rigid option for sport.</p><p><b>Piggyback</b>: high-Dk soft lens under a corneal RGP for comfort, centration or to protect an apical bearing area.</p><p><b>Hybrid</b>: RGP centre with soft skirt (e.g. SynergEyes-type designs; check local supply): RGP optics with soft comfort.</p>` },
  { topic: 'care', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'Name a soft lens MPS, its active ingredients and their purpose. Is it normal to have several actives?',
    model: `<p>Opti-Free PureMoist: Polyquad 0.001% (mainly antibacterial), Aldox 0.0006% (antifungal, some activity against Acanthamoeba trophozoites), HydraGlyde (wetting), citrate/borate buffers and cleaning agents. Yes: combining disinfectants widens the spectrum while keeping each concentration low enough to be biocompatible. Others with dual disinfectants: Biotrue (Polyquad + PHMB), Acuvue RevitaLens (Polyquad + alexidine).</p>` },
  { topic: 'care', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'Why is rub and rinse important?',
    model: `<p>Friction removes loosely bound deposits and the large majority of adherent micro-organisms (and Acanthamoeba) before disinfection, so the disinfectant faces a smaller load. "No-rub" claims fail in real-world compliance. Rub each side (~10–20 s depending on product) and rinse.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'Describe CLARE, CLPU and infiltrative keratitis, and how they differ from MK.',
    model: `<p><b>CLARE</b>: acute red painful eye on waking after overnight wear; diffuse infiltrates, no significant staining; Gram-negative endotoxins; resolves 24–72 h; ~50% recur. <b>CLPU</b>: small round peripheral anterior-stromal infiltrate with overlying staining, Gram-positive exotoxins, sterile, mild pain. <b>Infiltrative keratitis</b>: small infiltrates, mild symptoms, from solutions, poor fit, toxins. <b>MK</b>: central/paracentral, large epithelial defect, excavation, AC reaction, severe pain, discharge → urgent referral, cultures, fortified antibiotics.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'What is SEAL and how do you manage it?',
    model: `<p>Superior epithelial arcuate lesion: a split in the superior epithelium 2–3 mm from the limbus, from a stiff (high-modulus) lens pressed by a tight upper lid, often in SiHy wearers. Usually asymptomatic. Manage: stop wear until healed, refit with lower modulus or different edge/BC, avoid going back to low-Dk hydrogel.</p>` },

  // ---------------- CL orals study doc (Lydia's table, RGP design, troubleshooting, CIEs, drugs)
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Orals doc · Lydia', q: 'What lens would you give for each cornea/Rx combination, and why?',
    model: `<table class="t"><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Lens</th><th>Why</th></tr><tr><td>Spherical Rx, spherical cornea</td><td>Soft sphere (or spherical RGP)</td><td>Simplest fit; RGP for optics/fewer inflammatory events</td></tr><tr><td>Toric cornea, corneal cyl &lt;2 D</td><td>Soft toric first, or spherical RGP (&lt;1 D on flat K; 1–2 D flat K + ⅓ΔK)</td><td>Tear lens masks corneal cyl</td></tr><tr><td>Corneal cyl &gt;2 D, refractive ≈ 1.2–1.4 × corneal</td><td>Back-surface toric</td><td>Its induced cyl corrects the extra refractive cyl</td></tr><tr><td>Corneal cyl &gt;2 D, refractive = corneal</td><td>SPE bitoric</td><td>Front cyl only corrects induced cyl, so rotation doesn\'t matter</td></tr><tr><td>Corneal cyl &gt;2 D, refractive ≠ corneal</td><td>CPE bitoric</td><td>Front cyl corrects induced + residual; relies on the back toric lock</td></tr><tr><td>Spherical cornea, toric Rx (all internal)</td><td>Soft toric (usually) or front-surface toric RGP with prism</td><td>Spherical back has nothing to lock onto</td></tr><tr><td>Toric cornea, spherical Rx</td><td>Soft sphere</td><td>Drapes, so corneal and internal cyl keep cancelling</td></tr></table>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'Worked BST example: spec +1.75/−5.75×180, Ks 42.00 @180, 46.00 @90. Why does a back-surface toric work here?',
    model: `<p>Corneal cyl −4.00×180; refractive −5.75×180; ratio 5.75 / 4.00 ≈ 1.43 (in the 1.2–1.4 range). Residual = −1.75×180. A BST fitted to the toric cornea induces extra cyl because lens (n ≈ 1.47) and tears (n 1.336) differ in index: induced cyl ≈ 0.45–0.5 × back-surface toricity ≈ −2.00×180 here, which covers the residual. Order the BOZRs from Ks ± Mandell–Moore, BVP from the flat meridian with SAM-FAP; no front cyl needed. If the ratio doesn\'t fit, go bitoric (SPE or CPE).</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'Corneal, residual and refractive cyl: a power-cross example. Ks 7.85 @90 and 7.90 @180; refraction cyl −1.50×180.',
    model: `<p>Corneal cyl: steeper radius (7.85) is at 90, so −0.25×180 (as a rule the minus-cyl axis sits along the flatter, larger-radius meridian). Refractive cyl = corneal + residual → residual = −1.50×180 − (−0.25×180) = <b>−1.25×180</b>. That is what a spherical RGP would leave. Average physiological residual is about −0.50×90 (ATR).</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'When would you NOT choose a high-Dk rigid material?',
    model: `<ul><li>Ex-PMMA wearers who handle lenses roughly (high-Dk materials scratch, warp and break more easily).</li><li>Ultra-thin designs: use a stronger, lower-Dk material and let the thinness (t) carry the Dk/t.</li><li>Wettability or deposit problems with a particular high-Dk polymer.</li><li>Flexure-prone designs on toric corneas (softer high-Dk materials flex more).</li></ul><p>Use hyper-Dk (e.g. Boston XO2, Menicon Z) for overnight Ortho-K, sclerals and hypoxia-prone patients.</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'What are the signs of a failing spherical RGP fit on a toric cornea, and what can you do?',
    model: `<p><b>Signs</b>: lens rocks along the flat meridian, decentres, 3 & 9 staining, flexure (over-K shows residual cyl), variable vision. <b>Fixes</b>: steeper BOZR, smaller BOZD, aspheric design, larger TD, or move to a back-surface toric / bitoric.</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'RGP troubleshooting: position, staining, flare, early and late VA problems.',
    model: `<table class="t"><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Think about</th></tr><tr><td>Rides high / low / lateral, falls out, no movement</td><td>Curvature, size, zones, weight (thickness, lenticulation), junctions. ATR corneas → lateral decentration</td></tr><tr><td>Staining</td><td>Edge lift (peripheral curves), fit; patient MGD/dry eye</td></tr><tr><td>Haloes / flare</td><td>Optimise fit first, then BOZD vs pupil size</td></tr><tr><td>Early VA problems</td><td>Residual cyl (over-refract), wrong power, flexure, keratoconus</td></tr><tr><td>Late VA problems</td><td>Warpage (verify the lens), wrong lens worn, deposits (Progent, polish)</td></tr></table><p>Other: insufficient tear exchange → flatten BC/peripheral curves, blend junctions; seal-off → flatten peripheral curves; dimple veil → adjust fit; adherence → refit, lubricants.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'Oxygen criteria: Holden–Mertz and Harvitt–Bonanno.',
    model: `<ul><li><b>Holden–Mertz (1984)</b>: Dk/t 24 to avoid daytime oedema; 34 to limit overnight swelling to 8% with full daytime recovery; 87 to limit overnight swelling to 4% (same as no lens).</li><li><b>Harvitt–Bonanno (1999)</b>: Dk/t 35 to avoid stromal anoxia during the day; 125 to avoid anoxia anywhere in the cornea overnight.</li></ul><p>Striae appear at ~5% swelling, folds at ~8–10%.</p>` },
  { topic: 'fitting', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'How do you grade the tear film lipid layer, and what dry eye tests do you use?',
    model: `<p><b>Lipid patterns</b> (interferometry/Tearscope, low illumination to avoid reflex tearing): 1a open meshwork (thin), 1b closed meshwork, 2 amorphous (ideal), 3 flow/wave, 4 coloured fringes (thick, unstable).</p><p><b>Tests</b>: NIBUT/TBUT (&lt;10 s suspicious), tear meniscus height (~0.2–0.3 mm normal; ≤0.1 mm low), phenol red thread (15 s; &lt;10 mm low), Schirmer (5 min; &lt;10 mm low), osmolarity, staining, MG expression, questionnaires (OSDI, DEQ-5, CLDEQ, McMonnies).</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'Mucus strands/balls vs lens deposits: how do they differ?',
    model: `<table class="t"><tr><th></th><th>Mucin / mucus</th><th>Protein deposits</th></tr><tr><td>Lens</td><td>More with SiHy and RGP (stiffer, lipophilic surfaces)</td><td>More with ionic high-water hydrogels (lysozyme is positively charged)</td></tr><tr><td>Effect</td><td>Pulling strands irritates the surface; mucin balls leave pits that pool fluorescein</td><td>Denatured protein drives CLPC; reduced wettability and vision</td></tr><tr><td>Fix</td><td>Lower modulus, lubricants, blink</td><td>Rub and rinse, protein remover, more frequent replacement, DD, non-ionic or SiHy</td></tr></table>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'Papillae vs follicles: how do you tell them apart?',
    model: `<p><b>Papillae</b>: vascular response with surrounding oedema, a central vessel in each mound, on the tarsal conjunctiva: allergy, CLPC, mechanical irritation. <b>Follicles</b>: lymphoid aggregates, avascular dome with vessels around the base, often in the inferior fornix: viral, chlamydial, drug toxicity.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'What are the phases of corneal epithelial wound healing, and why do you wait before restarting lenses?',
    model: `<ol><li><b>Latent</b> (hours): cells retract and remodel; MMP-9 rises; gap junctions open.</li><li><b>Migration</b> (24–48 h): cells slide to cover the defect; tight junctions not yet sealed.</li><li><b>Proliferation</b> (days): the monolayer multiplies and stratifies, but cells are loosely attached.</li><li><b>Reattachment</b>: hemidesmosomes re-anchor to the basement membrane: days if it is intact, weeks to months if damaged (why RCEs recur).</li></ol><p>Full turnover is 7–10 days, so review at a week before going back to lenses.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'Microbial keratitis: signs, first-line regimen, and when to refer for fortified antibiotics.',
    model: `<p><b>Signs</b>: large (&gt;1 mm), irregular, central/paracentral infiltrate with epithelial excavation, AC reaction ± hypopyon, lid oedema, discharge, severe pain, ↓VA, no relief on lens removal. P. aeruginosa is the commonest CL organism (proteases, biofilm).</p><p><b>First line</b>: stop wear, fluoroquinolone (ciprofloxacin/ofloxacin 0.3%) q15 min for 6 h, q30 min rest of day, hourly day 2, then q4h until re-epithelialised; cycloplegic (cyclopentolate 1% BID or atropine) for pain; oral analgesia; culture lens, case and solution. Never patch, never a bandage lens. Chloramphenicol doesn\'t cover Pseudomonas.</p><p><b>Refer for fortified</b> (tobramycin 1.3–1.5% + cefazolin 5%, alternating) if deep, large, delayed presentation, in the visual axis, or not improving at 24–48 h. Acanthamoeba: pain out of proportion, radial keratoneuritis, ring infiltrate, water exposure, no steroids.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'What did the SCUT trial show about steroids in bacterial keratitis?',
    model: `<p>Steroids for Corneal Ulcers Trial (Srinivasan 2012; 2014 follow-up): adjunctive prednisolone phosphate 1% after 48 h of moxifloxacin gave <b>no overall difference</b> in BSCVA at 3 months and no increase in adverse events. Subgroups that may benefit: poor initial VA (count fingers or worse), large central ulcers covering the pupil, deep ulcers, and starting early (2–3 days). Avoid in Nocardia. Organisms: ~50% S. pneumoniae, ~25% P. aeruginosa, ~10% Nocardia (mostly India). Practical rule: only once culture is known and the antibiotic is working.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'CLPC: grading, sources and management.',
    model: `<p><b>Sources</b>: mechanical (stiff lens, edge; localised CLPC with RGP/SiHy), deposits/immune (generalised CLPC with hydrogels, atopy), EW, long wearing time, larger lenses, MGD.</p><p><b>Manage</b>: document grade, size, redness and zones (1–3, upper tarsus). Stop or reduce wear (symptoms ease in 2–5 days; signs take weeks to months), cold compresses and saline, review compliance, add a protein step, refit: DD, lower modulus, different material, shorter wear. Drugs for moderate–severe: olopatadine 0.1% or ketotifen 0.025% BID (olopatadine about as effective as FML in CLPC), short steroid course (FML 0.1% QID ~1 week, taper). Wear can restart before full resolution once symptoms settle.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'SICS and a peroxide splash: how do you recognise and manage each?',
    model: `<p><b>SICS</b>: diffuse or annular punctate staining near the limbus, bilateral, peaks ~2 h after insertion, MPS (esp. biguanide) + SiHy combinations. Manage: stop wear briefly, lubricants, change to a preservative-free system (peroxide) or DD, reteach.</p><p><b>Unneutralised peroxide</b>: immediate severe stinging, redness, tearing, diffuse staining. Irrigate profusely, treat as a mild chemical injury (lubricants, short FML course 1–2 days QID if needed), reteach the case and 6 h rule.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'Corneal erosion in a CL wearer: management.',
    model: `<p>Bright staining, sometimes negative staining at a raised edge; no infiltrate; moderate–severe pain, watering. Stop CLs, prophylactic antibiotic (e.g. ciprofloxacin QID) until re-epithelialised + 3 days, cycloplegic (cyclopentolate 1% BID) and/or topical NSAID (ketorolac 0.5% QID) or oral analgesia for pain, review in 24 h. No steroid (no infiltrate), no patching, no bandage lens in a CL wearer.</p>` },
  { topic: 'care', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'Steroid potency ranking and the cautions to know.',
    model: `<p>Pred Forte (prednisolone acetate 1%) &gt; dexamethasone (Maxidex 0.1%) &gt; Flarex (fluorometholone acetate 0.1%) &gt; Pred Minims 0.5% &gt; FML (fluorometholone alcohol 0.1%) / Flucon &gt; Hycor (hydrocortisone 1%). IOP-raising potential tracks potency. Acetate (lipophilic) penetrates intact epithelium better than phosphate. Cautions: HSV history, glaucoma/steroid responders (~1 in 3), long-term use → PSC cataract. Taper longer after stronger drugs, longer courses and more frequent dosing.</p>` },
  { topic: 'care', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'What do the common preservatives and disinfectants in CL solutions do?',
    model: `<ul><li><b>BAK</b>: binds to soft lenses and destabilises the tear film; not used with soft lenses.</li><li><b>Chlorhexidine</b>: never used alone (weak antifungal, binds to lenses).</li><li><b>Thiomersal</b>: antifungal; classic cause of CL-SLK and delayed hypersensitivity, largely abandoned.</li><li><b>Polyquad</b>: antibacterial, large molecule so less uptake into lenses.</li><li><b>Aldox</b>: antifungal, some anti-Acanthamoeba trophozoite activity.</li><li><b>PHMB</b>: biguanide; SICS with some SiHys.</li></ul><p>Combinations: Polyquad alone = Opti-Free; + Aldox = PureMoist/Replenish; + alexidine = RevitaLens; + PHMB = Biotrue.</p>` },

  // ---------------- Slide exam notes
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Slide exam notes', q: 'Microcysts vs vacuoles on the slit lamp, and when do microcysts need action?',
    model: `<p>Use high magnification and marginal retro-illumination. <b>Microcysts</b> (15–50 µm, dust-like specks, mid-peripheral annulus) show <b>reversed</b> illumination (light side away from the light source): higher index than the epithelium. <b>Vacuoles</b> are unreversed (look like bubbles). Intervene if &gt;50 microcysts or they break through the surface: stop lenses, refit with less hypoxic stress (high-Dk SiHy, daily wear). Expect a rebound increase for ~1 week before they clear.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Slide exam notes', q: 'Corneal infiltrates: when are they sterile and how do you manage sterile infiltrates?',
    model: `<p>Grey-white, 0.5–2 mm, round, usually separated from the limbus by a clear zone, epithelial/subepithelial; EW ≫ daily wear; rare with RGPs. Overlying staining → assume infection and treat/refer. <b>Sterile</b>: exclude infection, remove CLs, lubricants/saline rinses/cold compresses; if symptomatic or severe add a mild steroid (Flarex 0.1% QID) with antibiotic cover, review 24–48 h, cycloplegic if AC reaction, treat blepharitis. Resume with fresh lenses, ideally DD, once resolved (1–2 weeks, up to 3 months).</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Slide exam notes', q: 'Corneal neovascularisation in a CL wearer: what is significant and what do you do?',
    model: `<p>Spikes or buds from the limbal arcades and any vessel growth beyond the translucent limbal zone (&gt;1–1.5 mm) is significant. Draw location/extent/depth, grade (CCLRU), photograph. Stop wear, remove predisposing factors (hypoxia, tight fit, EW), restart cautiously with high Dk/t and better fit, watch for refilling of ghost vessels.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Slide exam notes', q: 'MGD in a CL wearer: signs and home management instructions.',
    model: `<p><b>Signs</b>: capped or blocked orifices, cloudy or toothpaste secretion, frothing, debris, short TBUT, lid margin hyperaemia and thickening, greasy lens deposits; unexplained CL intolerance. <b>Home</b>: warm compresses (hold until cool, repeat) then lid massage; lid scrubs (diluted baby shampoo or commercial wipes such as Sterilid/Ocusoft, along the lash line), twice daily for 2 weeks, then review. In-office expression or thermal treatment for severe cases. PF lubricants (lipid-based, e.g. Systane Balance) help evaporative dry eye.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Slide exam notes', q: 'Corneal foreign body: assessment and management.',
    model: `<p>Classify metallic/non-metallic, embedded/superficial, penetrating/non-penetrating. Seidel test, evert lids and sweep fornices, record size, depth and location. Irrigate or remove at the slit lamp, remove rust ring. Treat as an abrasion: topical antibiotic, analgesia (NSAID/cycloplegic), no CL until healed. Review 24 h, 3 days, 5–7 days (~12% RCE risk).</p>` },

  // ---------------- Lily's commonly asked list and Narelle's list
  { topic: 'rx', source: 'Lily · commonly asked', q: '"Why do you vertex BOTH the sphere and the cyl?"',
    model: `<p>Each principal meridian is a separate power, and effective power depends on the power: the higher meridian changes more. For −6.00/−2.00: −6.00 → −5.60, −8.00 → −7.30, so cyl −2.00 becomes −1.70. Vertexing only the sphere leaves the cyl wrong. No calculator: use the rule of thumb (±4–6 D change 0.25, 6–8 D 0.50, 8–10 D 0.75). Only apply nasal torque when you have to round the axis to one that\'s made.</p>` },
  { topic: 'specialty', source: 'Lily · commonly asked', q: '"Is this patient suitable for Ortho-K? What do you want to see on topography?"',
    model: `<p>Myopia up to about −4.00/−5.00 D (more with specialty designs), corneal toricity ≤1.50 D WTR (less ATR), astigmatism not limbus-to-limbus (or use a toric design), normal-range Ks (roughly 41–46 D), regular topography with a normal e-value (e ~0.5; higher e gives more flattening room), pupil not too large (haloes), normal lid tension, healthy surface, good compliance. Check: topography, HVID, pupil size, lids, tear film.</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Lily · commonly asked', q: '"What RGP information do you need, and how do toric RGPs stabilise?"',
    model: `<p>Need: topography (corneal toricity and whether it is limbus to limbus, e-value, Ks, irregularity), HVID (TD), pupil size (BOZD), lid position and tension, tear film. Stabilisation: back-surface torics and bitorics lock onto the toric cornea; front-surface torics need 1–1.5Δ base-down prism ± truncation. Fit bitorics with Mandell–Moore factors.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Orals notes · Narelle', q: 'What are the two main mechanisms of dry eye, and how do they matter in CL wear?',
    model: `<p><b>Aqueous-deficient</b> (Sjögren and non-Sjögren: age, meds, lacrimal disease) and <b>evaporative</b> (MGD most commonly, blink problems, lid exposure, CL wear itself). The lens splits the tear film into thin pre- and post-lens layers; the pre-lens film thins and evaporates quickly, so both mechanisms show up as end-of-day dryness. Manage the cause (MGD treatment, PF lubricants), then the lens (DD, wettable SiHy, less wear time, peroxide care).</p>` },
  { topic: 'presbyopia', source: 'Orals notes · Narelle', q: 'When would you use monovision, RGP monovision, overspecs or a toric multifocal?',
    model: `<ul><li><b>Monovision</b>: low adds (up to ~+1.50/+2.00), clear dominance, no critical stereo or night-driving demands.</li><li><b>RGP monovision</b>: high corneal cyl (especially oblique) where soft toric MFs perform poorly, existing RGP wearer.</li><li><b>Overspecs</b>: tasks beyond the lens (high add, prolonged reading, critical driving), uncorrected residual cyl.</li><li><b>Toric MF</b> (Biofinity toric MF to +2.50 add; Proclear MF Toric higher): cyl ≥0.75–1.00 D with an add.</li></ul><p>"Getting away with uncorrected cyl": put the full correction in the dominant (distance) eye and accept small uncorrected cyl in the near eye.</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Orals notes · Narelle', q: 'Static vs dynamic RGP fit assessment: what do you look at?',
    model: `<p><b>Dynamic</b> (loose/tight): centration, movement with blink (~1–2 mm), speed of recovery, lid attachment, position in primary and versions. <b>Static</b> (steep/flat, lens–cornea relationship): fluorescein with cobalt blue + Wratten filter after centring the lens: apical clearance (central pooling, possible bubble) = steep; central touch with wide peripheral pooling = flat; alignment = thin even film with a 0.4–0.5 mm edge band. Toric cornea + spherical lens → dumbbell along the flat meridian.</p>` },

  // ---------------- Gaps filled from a full re-read of every source document (Oct 2026)
  // Past-paper booklet (Vinod)
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q2', q: 'Reading an RGP specification: how do BOZD, ICW, PCW and LD relate, and what are the two ways to make a lens bigger?',
    model: `<p>LD = BOZD + 2 × ICW + 2 × PCW (ICD = BOZD + 2 × ICW). Radii get flatter outwards: PCR > ICR > BOZR.</p><ol><li><b>Keep the BOZD, widen the peripheral curves</b>: same central fit, wider edge band, little effect on stability.</li><li><b>Enlarge the BOZD, keep the peripheral system</b>: the steeper back optic zone now sits over flatter cornea, so the lens fits <b>steeper</b>. To keep the same fluorescein pattern, flatten the BOZR 0.05 mm for every 0.5 mm increase in BOZD.</li></ol>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q9', q: 'Worked front-surface toric RGP: OS −2.50/−1.25×090, Ks 43.00 @090 / 43.25 @180. Diagnostic lens 43.25 D, −3.00 D; over-refraction +0.25/−1.00×090. What do you order?',
    model: `<p>Corneal cyl 0.25 D (−0.25×090), so residual cyl = −1.25 − (−0.25) = <b>−1.00×090</b>: low corneal, significant residual → FST.</p><p>BVP = diagnostic −3.00 + over-refraction +0.25/−1.00×090 = −2.75/−1.00×090. FST cyl goes on the front, so write it in <b>plus-cyl</b> form: <b>−3.75/+1.00×180</b>.</p><p>Stabilise with ~1Δ base-down prism (dot the base) and expect 10–15° nasal rotation: in the LE that is to your left, so <b>Left Add</b> → order ×015.</p><p>Prism adds thickness: extra CT = prism × OAD / 100 = 1 × 9.0 / 100 = 0.09 mm, so a 0.15 mm lens becomes ~0.24 mm. Peripheral curves: SCR ≈ BOZR + 1 mm, PCR ≈ SCR + 2 mm.</p><p>In practice a soft toric is usually easier for this patient.</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q9', q: 'Worked back-surface toric: ocular Rx plano/−3.75×090, Ks 42.00 @090 / 44.75 @180. Why does a BST work here?',
    model: `<p>Corneal cyl = 2.75 D ATR (−2.75×090). Residual = −3.75 − (−2.75) = <b>−1.00×090</b>.</p><p>Mandell–Moore BOZRs: flat 0.25 D flatter (41.75 D, 8.08 mm), steep 0.75 D flatter (44.00 D, 7.67 mm). Back-surface ΔK = 2.25 D, so induced cyl ≈ 0.5 × 2.25 ≈ <b>−1.12 D</b>, which roughly matches the −1.00 D residual. That is the BST condition: refractive ≈ 1.2–1.4 × corneal (3.75 / 2.75 = 1.36).</p><p>Only one BVP is specified (along the flat meridian, SAM-FAP): plano + 0.25 = <b>+0.25 D</b>. OAD 9.2, BOZD 7.8, SCR/PCR 1 mm and 3 mm flatter than each BOZR.</p><p>Remba factors are an alternative to Mandell–Moore (e.g. 3.00 D: 0.25 flatter / 0.50 flatter).</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Past paper · Vinod Q12', q: 'Why do high-plus RGPs ride low, and how do you fix them?',
    model: `<p>A plus lens is heavy, with its centre of gravity forward, and has a thin edge: the upper lid has nothing to grip (no watermelon-seed effect) and the lower lid gives no support, so the lens drops. Results: poor tear exchange, flare from the optic zone edge, corneal distortion, 3 & 9 or inferior staining.</p><p>Fixes: <b>minus-carrier lenticulation (myoflange)</b> so the upper lid can grip the edge; <b>larger TD</b> for lid attachment; <b>steeper BOZR / apical clearance</b> for better centration; thinner, lighter lens (smaller front optic zone, lower specific-gravity material).</p>` },
  // Practice doc
  { topic: 'history', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'Indications for contact lenses over spectacles. How can contact lenses help nystagmus?',
    model: `<p><b>Refractive/optical</b>: high myopia or hyperopia (image size, field, aberrations, ring scotoma/diplopia), anisometropia, irregular astigmatism (keratoconus, grafts, scars, post-refractive surgery), aphakia. <b>Other</b>: cosmesis, sport, heavy frames, presbyopia, therapeutic (bandage lenses), amblyopia (occlusion lenses).</p><p><b>Nystagmus</b>: CLs move with the eye, so the patient always looks through the optical centre (no prismatic swing). Rigid lenses may also reduce the amplitude and frequency of the nystagmus, probably through lid/corneal sensory feedback. It varies from patient to patient.</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'Why do RGPs carry a lower infection risk than soft lenses? Which is safest: soft, RGP or Ortho-K?',
    model: `<p><b>RGPs</b>: no water, so organisms and preservatives aren't absorbed into the material; a tear pump of ~10–20% exchange per blink (soft ~1–2%) flushes debris; smaller lens, less deposit build-up, easier to clean. Daily-wear RGP has the lowest MK rate of any modality.</p><p><b>Ortho-K</b> is still overnight wear of a reverse-geometry, high-Dk lens, so the risk is higher than daily-wear RGP; most reported Ortho-K MK has been in children and with poor case hygiene, often Pseudomonas or Acanthamoeba. Among soft lenses, DDs don't lower the absolute MK rate much but cut the severity.</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'Monocurve or tricurve? How do you choose RGP total diameter and BOZD, and how do you measure pupil size?',
    model: `<p><b>Tricurve</b> (or multicurve/aspheric): the cornea flattens towards the periphery, so a monocurve would bear at the edge. Peripheral radii are flatter, i.e. <b>larger numbers</b> in mm.</p><p><b>TD</b>: smaller than HVID (≈ HVID − 2 mm; 11 mm HVID → ~9.2 mm), adjusted for palpebral aperture and lid position (wide aperture or lid attachment → larger, e.g. 9.6). <b>BOZD</b>: larger than the dim-light pupil (≈ pupil + 1 mm): <5 mm → 7.6, 5–7 → 7.8, >7 → 8.0.</p><p><b>Pupil size</b>: in dim light, with a slit-lamp beam of known height or a pupil gauge/ruler; compare the pupil with the beam length. Many topographers also report it.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'Which part of the cornea is most at risk of hypoxia, how is Dk/t quoted, and what is corneal exhaustion syndrome?',
    model: `<p><b>Central cornea</b> under a minus lens's thick centre (or a plus lens's centre); with prism-ballast torics the thick inferior zone gives regional hypoxia too. Oxygen demand is also high centrally.</p><p>Dk/t is the lens's oxygen transmissibility measured through the centre of a <b>−3.00 D</b> lens; it changes with power, so high plus and torics are lower than the quoted figure.</p><p><b>Corneal exhaustion syndrome</b>: sudden intolerance after years of low-Dk wear (often PMMA): discomfort, reduced VA, photophobia, oedema, marked endothelial polymegethism. Refit with high Dk or stop wear.</p>` },
  { topic: 'materials', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'If SiHy Dk rises as water falls, why do silicone hydrogels still need water? Why are most lenses now SiHy?',
    model: `<p>Water gives <b>ion permeability</b> (ionoflux): Na⁺ and water must pass through the lens to keep a hydrophilic post-lens tear layer, so the lens keeps moving and doesn't bind. It also helps wettability, flexibility and comfort. Very low-water early SiHys needed surface treatment and were stiff.</p><p>Most lenses fitted now are SiHy: high Dk/t (minimal oedema, limbal hyperaemia and neovascularisation), easy handling for new wearers, and lower water so less dehydration. Downsides: higher modulus (SEAL, localised CLPC), lipid deposits, and some solution-staining combinations.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'Is MK more common with silicone hydrogels than hydrogels? Do daily disposables lower the risk? What is the risk of sleeping in lenses?',
    model: `<p>SiHy did <b>not</b> reduce MK risk in overnight wear despite removing hypoxia, so infection isn't mainly an oxygen problem. Rates for SiHy and hydrogel EW are broadly similar.</p><p>DDs haven't been shown to lower the <b>absolute</b> risk of MK compared with daily-wear reusables, but they give <b>less severe</b> disease and less vision loss, and remove case and solution risks.</p><p><b>Overnight wear is the biggest risk factor</b>: roughly 5–10× daily wear. Australian figures (Stapleton 2008): about 2 per 10,000 per year for daily-wear soft lenses vs about 20–25 per 10,000 for overnight soft wear. Occasional overnight wear also raises the risk.</p>` },
  { topic: 'ew', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'Why not prescribe extended wear routinely, and what extra screening do you do if you do?',
    model: `<p>EW approvals come from trials with new lenses, new cases, strict compliance and close follow-up; real-world wearers don't behave like trial subjects. Overnight wear raises MK risk 5–10× and adds CLARE, CLPU, infiltrative keratitis, hypoxia and CLPC.</p><p>Screen out or be cautious with: immunocompromise, systemic or topical steroids, diabetes, dry eye (HRT, contraceptives, antidepressants, Sjögren's), blepharitis/MGD, poor hygiene, water exposure, dusty work, previous CIEs. Start with daily wear, then one night, review next morning, then build up. Educate on red-eye warning signs and keep spare specs.</p>` },
  { topic: 'care', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'How well do heat, peroxide and MPS work against bacteria, fungi and Acanthamoeba? What does the evidence on one-step and two-step peroxide say?',
    model: `<table class="t"><tr><th>Regimen</th><th>Bacteria</th><th>Fungi</th><th>Acanthamoeba</th></tr><tr><td>Heat, 10 min at 80 °C</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes (probably, incl. cysts)</td></tr><tr><td>Peroxide, &lt;1 h</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Peroxide, overnight</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No (?)</td></tr><tr><td>MPS, overnight</td><td>Yes</td><td>No (?)</td><td>No (?)</td></tr></table><p>Cysts are the problem: two-step peroxide (long 3% exposure before separate neutralisation) is more cysticidal than one-step disc/tablet systems, which neutralise within minutes. Rubbing (and alcohol-based cleaners such as Miraflow) physically removes cysts. Opti-Free PureMoist's Aldox has trophozoite activity only. Complete Moisture Plus was withdrawn in 2007 after a US AK outbreak.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Slide exam notes', q: 'Acanthamoeba keratitis: what does it look like, and why mustn\'t reusable lens wearers swim in their lenses?',
    model: `<p><b>Symptoms</b>: pain out of proportion to the signs, even early; photophobia, slow progression, often initially misdiagnosed as HSV. <b>Signs</b>: epitheliopathy or pseudodendrites, radial keratoneuritis (infiltrates along corneal nerves), later a ring infiltrate. Risk: water contact (tap, pool, hot tub, swimming), poor case hygiene, home-made saline.</p><p>Cysts survive MPS and one-step peroxide, so a lens that has been in water can't be reliably disinfected. That is why the answer to "can't I just clean it?" is no: DDs discarded after swimming, or Rx goggles. Refer urgently; no steroids early.</p>` },
  { topic: 'care', source: '2022 orals · Wednesday', q: 'Name two hydrogen peroxide systems. How do they differ, and when wouldn\'t you use peroxide?',
    model: `<p><b>AOSept Plus (with HydraGlyde)</b>: one-step, platinum disc in the case neutralises over ~6 h; works for soft lenses and RGPs. <b>Oxysept 1-Step</b>: catalase tablet that colours the solution when neutralisation is under way; labelled for soft lenses.</p><p><b>Pros</b>: preservative-free (good for sensitive or SICS-prone patients), no rubbing needed for disinfection, good bacterial and fungal kill. <b>Cons</b>: must neutralise fully (≥6 h) or it stings and burns (treat as a mild chemical injury); never rinse lenses with it or put it in the eye; less convenient for travel.</p><p><b>Avoid</b> for shift workers or irregular sleepers who can't give it 6 h, poor compliers, and patients who travel a lot. Older advice also avoided some high-water ionic hydrogels.</p>` },
  { topic: 'care', source: '2022 orals · Thursday', q: 'How do you choose between multipurpose solutions?',
    model: `<table class="t"><tr><th>MPS</th><th>Biocides</th><th>Min soak</th></tr><tr><td>Opti-Free PureMoist (Alcon)</td><td>Polyquad 0.001% + Aldox 0.0006%</td><td>6 h</td></tr><tr><td>Acuvue RevitaLens (J&J)</td><td>Polyquad + alexidine</td><td>6 h</td></tr><tr><td>Biotrue (B+L)</td><td>Polyquad + PHMB</td><td>4 h</td></tr><tr><td>renu Advanced (B+L)</td><td>Polyquad + alexidine + PHMB</td><td>4 h</td></tr></table><p>Choose on: efficacy (Aldox has some anti-Acanthamoeba trophozoite activity), <b>compatibility with the lens</b> (PHMB-based MPS with some SiHys gives more SICS), previous sensitivity, soak time vs the patient's routine, and the maker's instructions (some require a rinse before insertion, e.g. RevitaLens; follow the label rub/rinse times). Having two biocides is normal: each covers different organisms at lower, less toxic concentrations.</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: '2022 orals · Thursday', q: 'Boston XO vs XO2: what is different, and why is XO still so common? Are higher-Dk RGPs made thinner?',
    model: `<p><b>Boston XO</b> (hexafocon A): Dk 100, wetting angle 49° (captive bubble), Rockwell hardness 112, RI 1.415, SG 1.27. <b>Boston XO2</b> (hexafocon B): Dk 141, slightly softer.</p><p>XO is still widely used because it balances Dk with hardness, durability, dimensional stability and wettability, it's familiar, and many designs come in it. Choose higher Dk for overnight wear (Ortho-K), thick designs and sclerals.</p><p>Higher-Dk materials are softer and flex more, so they are <b>not</b> made thinner; if anything they're made a little thicker, which they can afford because of the high Dk. They also scratch and warp more easily and can wet less well, so avoid them for rough handlers (ex-PMMA wearers) and ultra-thin designs.</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: '2022 orals · Thursday', q: '"What if he wants to sleep in his RGPs?" What can go wrong?',
    model: `<p>Only approved high-Dk materials and designs (e.g. Ortho-K) should be worn overnight. Problems: <b>lens adherence</b> (thin, viscous post-lens tear film on waking; indentation ring, peripheral desiccation staining), central oedema and striae, MK (overnight wear multiplies the risk), CLARE and CLPU (less common than with soft), 3 & 9 staining, and corneal warpage. If you allow it: hyper-Dk material, a fit with good edge clearance, instruct to blink and nudge the lens on waking, use lubricants, review next morning.</p>` },
  { topic: 'lifestyle', source: '2022 orals · Thursday & Friday', q: 'Which organisms worry you with swimming, scuba diving and outdoor contact sport, and why?',
    model: `<ul><li><b>Water</b> (pool, sea, lakes, tap): <i>Acanthamoeba</i> (cysts resist disinfection), <i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i> and other Gram-negatives (the commonest cause of CL MK; protease and biofilm make it aggressive).</li><li><b>Soil and vegetative matter</b> (rugby, camping, gardening): <b>fungi</b> (Fusarium, Aspergillus), especially after corneal trauma.</li><li>Trauma from contact sport creates the epithelial break that lets them in.</li></ul><p>Hence: DDs discarded after water or dirty exposure, goggles, no water near reusables, and a low threshold to stop wearing and be seen.</p>` },
  { topic: 'history', source: '2022 orals · Thursday', q: '"Why do you want to know my age and gender?"',
    model: `<p>Young adult <b>males</b> are over-represented in CL MK and non-compliance (overnight wear, poor case hygiene, sport, smoking). Age also matters for presbyopia and residual accommodation, tear film and lid laxity in older patients, dexterity, and the likelihood of overnight wear or internet buying in younger patients. Say why you ask; don't let it sound like you're judging the patient.</p>` },
  { topic: 'presbyopia', source: '2022 orals · Friday', q: '"Are you just going to abandon the cyl?" When is a spherical (multifocal) lens OK for a patient with some cyl?',
    model: `<p>Spherical equivalent is reasonable when the cyl is small (≤0.75 D) or small relative to the sphere (a rough 4:1 sphere-to-cyl rule), the axis is not oblique, and the patient accepts slightly softer vision. SE doesn't "mask" cyl: it puts the circle of least confusion on the retina.</p><p>For +5.00/−1.25 the cyl is too much to drop for full-time wear: use a toric multifocal (Biofinity toric MF), monovision in daily torics, or distance torics with readers. For occasional, low-demand use (e.g. diving) a DD in spherical equivalent can be acceptable if you tell the patient.</p>` },
  { topic: 'presbyopia', source: '2022 orals · Friday', q: 'Which keeps binocularity better, monovision or multifocal CLs? What do you counsel a new presbyopic wearer about?',
    model: `<p><b>Multifocals</b> keep both eyes working together at all distances (better stereopsis and binocular summation), at the cost of contrast, glare and haloes. <b>Monovision</b> deliberately blurs one eye at each distance: reduced stereopsis and contrast, worse at night, poor for ball sports, pilots, surgeons and some drivers.</p><p>Counsel: vision will be a compromise, not "perfect" at every distance; allow 1–2 weeks to adapt; judge it in real life, not behind a phoropter; be careful driving at night at first (keep distance specs or overspecs); expect fine-tuning (±0.25 D over-refraction, D/N lens swaps); readers or overspecs are a back-up, not a failure.</p>` },
  { topic: 'specialty', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'Topography vs tomography. What do you look for when screening for keratoconus?',
    model: `<p><b>Placido topography</b>: anterior surface only; fast, sensitive to surface irregularity, less affected by opacities; good for fitting. <b>Tomography</b> (Scheimpflug/OCT): anterior and posterior surfaces plus pachymetry; posterior changes appear before anterior ones, so it detects early KC better.</p><p>Look for: an asymmetric or skewed bow-tie on the axial map, inferior–superior asymmetry, steep K over ~48 D, a thin and displaced thinnest point (and a big difference between the eyes), and posterior float elevation relative to a best-fit ellipsoid. Signs agreeing in the same location (pachymetry + posterior elevation) mean KC, so no LASIK. Stop soft lenses ~2 weeks and RGPs ~2–4 weeks before mapping, and take the scan before drops.</p>` },
  { topic: 'rgp', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'What is irregular astigmatism? Which lens would you choose for: WTR cyl with no sphere, ATR cyl, keratoconus, low sphere/high cyl with an add?',
    model: `<p><b>Irregular astigmatism</b>: principal meridians not 90° apart or curvature changes irregularly across the pupil (KC, grafts, scars, post-surgery). Spectacles and soft lenses can't correct it; a rigid lens replaces the irregular front surface with a smooth one and the tear lens fills the gaps.</p><ul><li>WTR cyl, little sphere: soft torics rotate and blur; RGP (bitoric if corneal cyl ≥2 D).</li><li>ATR cyl: soft torics stabilise well (thick zones sit under the lids); spherical RGPs decentre laterally on ATR corneas.</li><li>Irregular cornea / KC: RGP, then scleral, hybrid or piggyback; Kerasoft-type soft for intolerance.</li><li>Low sphere, high cyl, needs an add: RGP monovision with overspecs, or a custom toric multifocal.</li></ul>` },
  { topic: 'history', source: 'Orals doc', q: 'A patient sits down wanting lenses for an Rx of −3.75/−0.75×45. Walk me through the visit. And what do you ask a wearer at aftercare?',
    model: `<p><b>New fit</b>: history and motivation → confirm the refraction if needed → Ks/topography, HVID, pupils, lids, tear film → slit lamp with fluorescein and lid eversion → choose and insert a trial lens → settle 10–20 min → over-refract → assess fit (centration, coverage, movement, tightness, rotation for torics) → teach I&R and care → trial or dispense → review at about a week.</p><p><b>Aftercare questions</b>: which lens and solution, hours today and usually, how long they've worn CLs, replacement and case routine (ask them to show you), lapses (sleeping, water, topping up), past problems or stopping wear, end-of-day comfort, vision at distance and near, and "do you love your lenses?". Examine with the lens on, then off with fluorescein and lid eversion; grade findings.</p>` },
  { topic: 'fitting', source: 'Practice doc · Sulley et al. 2015', q: 'How do the common soft torics stabilise? Name the design for each.',
    model: `<ul><li><b>Acuvue Oasys for Astigmatism</b>: Accelerated Stabilisation Design (thick zones at 3 and 9 o'clock, thin top and bottom; dynamic stabilisation).</li><li><b>Air Optix for Astigmatism</b>: thickened areas around 4 and 8 o'clock (Precision Balance 8|4).</li><li><b>Biofinity toric / Avaira toric</b>: optimised (peri-)ballast, a wide inferior thick band with no prism in the optic zone.</li><li><b>clariti toric, PureVision toric</b>: prism ballast; <b>PureVision 2</b>: modified prism ballast.</li></ul><p>When a lens rocks or won't settle, switching to a different stabilisation family is often more useful than changing BC.</p>` },
  { topic: 'lenschoice', source: '2025 orals · Thursday', q: 'The Biofinity toric won\'t settle. Name other reusable soft torics and which can be slept in.',
    model: `<ul><li><b>Acuvue Oasys for Astigmatism</b> (J&J, 2-weekly SiHy): up to 6 nights EW. Goes to −2.75 DC in minus powers only.</li><li><b>Air Optix plus HydraGlyde for Astigmatism</b> (Alcon, monthly SiHy): up to 6 nights EW (confirm on the insert).</li><li><b>Total30 for Astigmatism</b> (Alcon, monthly SiHy): daily wear only.</li><li><b>ULTRA for Astigmatism</b> (B+L, monthly SiHy): check the insert before quoting EW.</li><li><b>clariti/cAIR toric</b> (CooperVision, 2-weekly SiHy): daily wear.</li></ul><p>Not EW: <b>Acuvue Moist</b> and <b>Biotrue ONEday</b> are daily-disposable <b>hydrogels</b> (Dk/t ~25–42). The only 30-night lens is <b>Air Optix Night & Day Aqua</b>, which is spherical.</p>` },
  { topic: 'presbyopia', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'Biofinity multifocal: how do you pick D and N lenses, and how do you enhance vision at the one-week review? How can you check MF centration?',
    model: `<table class="t"><tr><th>Add</th><th>Dominant eye</th><th>Non-dominant eye</th></tr><tr><td>+1.00</td><td>D</td><td>D</td></tr><tr><td>+1.50</td><td>D</td><td>D</td></tr><tr><td>+2.00</td><td>D</td><td>N</td></tr><tr><td>+2.50</td><td>D</td><td>N</td></tr></table><p>D = centre-distance, N = centre-near. Start from the vertexed spherical equivalent with the maximum plus, let the lenses settle ≥15 min, judge vision <b>binocularly</b> in real-world conditions. To enhance, use hand-held trial lenses (not a phoropter): ±0.25 D (up to ±0.50) on the distance power of the eye that needs it; for near, +0.50 on the add of the eye that needs it.</p><p><b>Centration</b>: compare a topography difference (tangential) map with and without the lens; the add zone should centre on the pupil/visual axis.</p>` },
  { topic: 'presbyopia', source: 'Practice doc', q: 'How do rigid multifocals work, and how are they different from soft multifocals?',
    model: `<ul><li><b>Aspheric</b> (usually centre-distance): curvature changes across the back or front surface so plus increases towards the edge; smooth D–I–N transition; little lid interaction needed.</li><li><b>Concentric</b> simultaneous designs: rings of D and N power in front of the pupil at once; depend on pupil size and lighting.</li><li><b>Translating (alternating)</b> bifocals/trifocals: a near segment at the bottom, prism-ballasted (sometimes truncated); the lower lid pushes the lens up in downgaze so the pupil looks through the add.</li></ul><p>Soft MFs are almost all <b>simultaneous</b> (concentric or aspheric): they can't translate because they're large and fixed under the lids. Translating RGPs give the sharpest near vision but need good lower-lid position. Examples: Capricornia, EyeSpace bespoke MFs.</p>` },
  { topic: 'complications', source: 'Orals doc · drugs', q: 'Chloramphenicol, tobramycin or ciprofloxacin for a contact lens wearer? Where do cycloplegics fit?',
    model: `<ul><li><b>Chloramphenicol</b> (0.5% drops, 1% ointment): bacteriostatic, 50S inhibitor, broad spectrum but <b>poor against Pseudomonas</b>, so not for CL-related keratitis. Avoid with personal/family history of blood dyscrasias.</li><li><b>Tobramycin</b> 0.3% (fortified 1.3–1.5%): aminoglycoside, 30S, bactericidal, good Gram-negative and Pseudomonas cover; epithelial toxicity.</li><li><b>Ciprofloxacin / ofloxacin</b> 0.3%: fluoroquinolones (DNA gyrase); first-line monotherapy for CL MK (q15 min loading dose, then q30 min, q1h, then q4h). Ofloxacin penetrates better; cipro can leave white precipitates. Caution with deep ulcers (MMP, melt).</li><li><b>Cycloplegics</b> (cyclopentolate 1% BD, atropine 1%): relieve ciliary spasm pain, prevent posterior synechiae and stabilise the blood–aqueous barrier in MK with AC reaction.</li></ul><p>Never patch or use a bandage lens on a CL complication.</p>` },
  { topic: 'care', source: 'Orals doc · drugs', q: 'Olopatadine vs ketotifen for CLPC and allergy in a lens wearer: how do they work and how are they used with lenses?',
    model: `<p>Both are dual-action <b>H1 antihistamine + mast-cell stabiliser</b> drops. <b>Olopatadine 0.1%</b> (Patanol, S4) is the more effective and was as effective as FML in controlling CLPC; <b>ketotifen 0.025%</b> (Zaditen, S3, available preservative-free unit dose). Sodium cromoglycate is a mast-cell stabiliser only.</p><p>With lenses: instil before insertion or after removal and wait 10–15 min before putting the lens in (BAK in preserved drops binds to soft lenses); use unpreserved forms where possible; move to DDs and continue monitoring for CLPC.</p>` },
];
